# OAuth login: a dedicated bind service account, and the group you must belong to to log in.
#
# Mirrors the production shape, where a TrustedApplications service account binds for the identity
# provider and a single "gate" group decides who may authenticate at all — separately from the
# app-ocp-rbac-* groups, which decide what you can DO once you are in.
#
# Apply with:  ./40-setup-oauth-ldap-login.sh bind-account
# or by hand:  oc exec -c openldap -n ldap-testing <pod> -- \
#                ldapmodify -c -x -D "cn=admin,dc=ephico2real,dc=com" -w admin123 -f <this file>
#
# ldapmodify, not ldapadd, and -c: the file is written to be re-runnable. The two `add` records fail
# harmlessly with "Already exists (68)" on a second run and -c carries on, then the `modify` records
# use `replace`, which reaches the same end state whether or not the values were already there.
#
# ── Why the gate group is groupOfUniqueNames and not groupOfNames ─────────────────────────────────
#
# An OpenShift LDAP identity provider performs ONE search, for the user, and the group restriction has
# to be a condition on the USER entry. That means the user needs a memberOf attribute, which OpenLDAP
# only maintains through the memberof overlay.
#
# This directory's overlay is already loaded and is configured — measured, not assumed — as:
#
#   olcOverlay={0}memberof,olcDatabase={1}mdb,cn=config
#     olcMemberOfGroupOC:  groupOfUniqueNames
#     olcMemberOfMemberAD: uniqueMember
#     olcMemberOfMemberOfAD: memberOf
#
# The app-ocp-rbac-* groups are groupOfNames with `member`, so the overlay never matches them and no
# user entry had ever had a memberOf. Creating THIS group as groupOfUniqueNames with `uniqueMember`
# matches what the overlay already watches, so memberOf appears on these members the moment this file
# is applied — no slapd reconfiguration, and nothing about the 41 existing RBAC groups changes.
#
# If you ever need to gate login on one of the app-ocp-rbac-* groups instead, the overlay has to be
# repointed at groupOfNames/member, and because the overlay does not backfill you must then re-write
# every group's member attribute for memberOf to appear. That is a bigger, riskier change; this file
# deliberately avoids needing it.
#
# ── Why a wildcard group filter does not work ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Measured on this directory:
#
#   (cn=app-ocp-rbac-*)                       41 matches   cn is a string attribute, has SUBSTR
#   (member=cn=john*,ou=People,...)            0 matches   member is DN-syntax, no SUBSTR rule
#   (member=uid=john.doe,ou=People,...)       14 matches   absolute DN
#
# A substring assertion against a DN-syntax attribute returns success with zero rows — no error. So a
# filter like (memberOf=cn=app-ocp-rbac-*,ou=Groups,...) silently denies everyone, which at a login
# prompt is indistinguishable from the directory being down. Group names must be ABSOLUTE, which is
# exactly what a real production config does. For several groups, OR them.
#
# Membership below is a deliberate SUBSET of the directory's users. A gate that everybody is inside
# proves nothing, so bob.wilson and charlie.brown are held out to keep the denial path testable —
# 7 of the 9 ou=People entries are in. It used to be 4 of 9 ("half are left out", as this line said
# for a while); dana.lee, sarah.jones and jeff were added so the dashboard has enough users with an
# OpenShift display name to be worth looking at. Two is the floor, not a target: see the note on the
# gate group itself for why those two specifically must stay out.

# ── Bind service account for the identity provider ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Separate from cn=ocp-ldap-bind-serviceid, which the group-sync operator uses. Two consumers, two
# credentials: either can be rotated or revoked without taking the other down.
#
# It is ALSO a member of the gate group below, so the same account both binds for the provider and
# serves as a login identity you can test with — one whose password is stored in a form the server
# accepts, which is not true of every user here. Measured across ou=People:
#
#   john.doe, jane.smith, bob.wilson,   userPassword: {SSHA}password123, from
#   alice.cooper, charlie.brown         ldap-structure-combined.ldif. That is a literal string behind an
#                                       {SSHA} prefix, not a hash: the base64 body is 11 bytes and
#                                       decodes to nothing usable, so slapd rejects EVERY bind for them.
#
#   lateef.o, dana.lee, sarah.jones     cleartext `newuser123`, from the later LDIFs. These bind fine,
#                                       and lateef.o is in the gate group below — so it is a working
#                                       console login with no extra setup.
#
# Give one of the malformed ones a usable password when you want a specific username:
#   ldappasswd -x -D "cn=admin,dc=ephico2real,dc=com" -w admin123 \
#     -s '<newpassword>' "uid=jane.smith,ou=People,dc=ephico2real,dc=com"
#
# extensibleObject is what allows uid on an organizationalRole entry. The provider searches for the
# login attribute (uid), so without it this account could not be found no matter what the filter said.
dn: cn=ocp-oauth-bind-serviceid,ou=TrustedApplications,dc=ephico2real,dc=com
changetype: add
objectClass: simpleSecurityObject
objectClass: organizationalRole
objectClass: extensibleObject
cn: ocp-oauth-bind-serviceid
uid: ocp-oauth-bind-serviceid
description: Bind account for the OpenShift OAuth LDAP identity provider, and a testable login identity
userPassword: oauthbindpassword123

# ── The login gate group ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Same name as production. Being in this group is what makes you able to log in to OpenShift at all;
# what you can do afterwards comes from the app-ocp-rbac-* groups the operator syncs.
#
# ── Who is in, and why it is not everybody ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# LOGGING IN IS WHAT CREATES THE OpenShift User OBJECT, and its fullName comes from the provider's
# `attributes.name: ["displayName", "cn"]` mapping. So this membership list decides which test users
# the group-sync-dashboard can show a display name for — nothing else populates it, and group
# membership alone populates nothing. Measured after adding dana.lee, sarah.jones and jeff and
# logging in as each: 7 of the 10 distinct group members now carry a fullName, 3 do not.
#
# The cn fallback is live and worth knowing: dana.lee, sarah.jones and jeff have NO displayName in
# this directory, and OpenShift filled fullName from cn — "Dana Lee", "Sarah Jones", "jeff bush".
# So a fullName here may be a tidy display name, a login-shaped one, or (ocp-oauth-bind-serviceid)
# just the uid again.
#
# STILL DELIBERATELY OUT, and do not "fix" this:
#   bob.wilson, charlie.brown   the denial-path fixtures. Both are group members with no User
#                               object, which is what keeps the dashboard's "no display name" state
#                               reachable — add them and every member is named, and the case stops
#                               being testable. bob.wilson is also the better GATE-denial subject
#                               specifically because his LDAP password is unusable-then-fixable by
#                               hand (see the passwords note below), which is how you tell a gate
#                               refusal from a bad credential.
#   hello1                      a group member with no ou=People entry at all, so it cannot be
#                               added here and can never acquire a name. A third distinct state
#                               from "has not logged in yet".
#
# dana.lee, sarah.jones and jeff needed no ldappasswd: all three already carry cleartext
# `newuser123` from the later LDIFs, unlike the five with the malformed {SSHA} value below.
dn: cn=app-ssb-autobahnusers,ou=Groups,dc=ephico2real,dc=com
changetype: add
objectClass: groupOfUniqueNames
cn: app-ssb-autobahnusers
description: Gate group - membership is required to authenticate to OpenShift
uniqueMember: uid=john.doe,ou=People,dc=ephico2real,dc=com
uniqueMember: uid=jane.smith,ou=People,dc=ephico2real,dc=com
uniqueMember: uid=alice.cooper,ou=People,dc=ephico2real,dc=com
uniqueMember: uid=lateef.o,ou=People,dc=ephico2real,dc=com
uniqueMember: uid=dana.lee,ou=People,dc=ephico2real,dc=com
uniqueMember: uid=sarah.jones,ou=People,dc=ephico2real,dc=com
uniqueMember: uid=jeff,ou=People,dc=ephico2real,dc=com
uniqueMember: cn=ocp-oauth-bind-serviceid,ou=TrustedApplications,dc=ephico2real,dc=com

# ── Re-run safety ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `replace` reaches the intended state whether or not the adds above did anything, so an existing
# directory converges instead of being left half-updated.
dn: cn=ocp-oauth-bind-serviceid,ou=TrustedApplications,dc=ephico2real,dc=com
changetype: modify
replace: objectClass
objectClass: simpleSecurityObject
objectClass: organizationalRole
objectClass: extensibleObject
-
replace: uid
uid: ocp-oauth-bind-serviceid
-
replace: userPassword
userPassword: oauthbindpassword123

dn: cn=app-ssb-autobahnusers,ou=Groups,dc=ephico2real,dc=com
changetype: modify
replace: uniqueMember
uniqueMember: uid=john.doe,ou=People,dc=ephico2real,dc=com
uniqueMember: uid=jane.smith,ou=People,dc=ephico2real,dc=com
uniqueMember: uid=alice.cooper,ou=People,dc=ephico2real,dc=com
uniqueMember: uid=lateef.o,ou=People,dc=ephico2real,dc=com
uniqueMember: uid=dana.lee,ou=People,dc=ephico2real,dc=com
uniqueMember: uid=sarah.jones,ou=People,dc=ephico2real,dc=com
uniqueMember: uid=jeff,ou=People,dc=ephico2real,dc=com
uniqueMember: cn=ocp-oauth-bind-serviceid,ou=TrustedApplications,dc=ephico2real,dc=com

# ── Usable passwords for the human gate members ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LAB ONLY. These are test-directory credentials in a repo that also carries admin123 and
# bindpassword123 — nothing here is a real secret, and nothing here should ever be pointed at a real
# directory.
#
# Why this is needed at all: ldap-structure-combined.ldif sets `userPassword: {SSHA}password123` for
# john.doe, jane.smith, bob.wilson, alice.cooper and charlie.brown. That is not a hash — it is the
# literal string `password123` behind an {SSHA} prefix, an 11-byte base64 body that decodes to nothing
# usable — so slapd rejects EVERY bind for them, with nothing in the logs to say why. Three of those
# five are gate members, so without this the gate would allow them and the login would still fail,
# which is the most confusing possible combination to debug.
#
# Set as cleartext, exactly as lateef.o and the service accounts already are: slapd accepts it and it
# stays greppable, which is what you want in a lab and never anywhere else.
#
# bob.wilson and charlie.brown are deliberately left alone. They are NOT in the gate group, so leaving
# their passwords unusable would confuse a denial test — you could not tell a gate denial from a bad
# credential. bob.wilson is the better denial subject precisely because he can bind to LDAP and is
# still refused by OpenShift; give him one by hand when you want that test:
#   ldappasswd -x -D "cn=admin,dc=ephico2real,dc=com" -w admin123 \
#     -s 'GateTest123!' "uid=bob.wilson,ou=People,dc=ephico2real,dc=com"
dn: uid=john.doe,ou=People,dc=ephico2real,dc=com
changetype: modify
replace: userPassword
userPassword: Ldap123!

dn: uid=jane.smith,ou=People,dc=ephico2real,dc=com
changetype: modify
replace: userPassword
userPassword: Ldap123!

dn: uid=alice.cooper,ou=People,dc=ephico2real,dc=com
changetype: modify
replace: userPassword
userPassword: Ldap123!
